<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section>
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the following system of linear equations:
        <md alignment="align">
          <mrow>x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3} + x_{4} \amp= 1,</mrow>
          <mrow>x_{2} + x_{4} \amp= 1,</mrow>
          <mrow>x_{3} \amp= 1</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Write the coefficient and augmented matrix of the system.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Write the system as a linear equation with vector coefficients.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Write the system in matrix-vector form.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Which is your favorite?</p></statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <p>Part (a) we have done many times before, but let's do it once more now the coefficient matrix has more significance. Here is the coefficient matrix:
    <md>\begin{pmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1  \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
    \end{pmatrix}</md>
  </p>
  <p>For the augmented matrix,
    <md>\begin{pmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1
    \end{pmatrix}</md>
  </p>

  <p>For part (b), we take the columns from the coefficient matrix and add them up with coefficients given by the variables to equal the last column of the augmented matrix.
    <md>x_{1}\begin{pmatrix}
      1 \\ 0 \\ 0
    \end{pmatrix} + x_{2}\begin{pmatrix}
      1 \\ 1 \\ 0
    \end{pmatrix} + x_{3}\begin{pmatrix}
      1 \\ 0 \\ 1
    \end{pmatrix} + x_{4}\begin{pmatrix}
      1 \\ 1 \\ 0
    \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
      1 \\ 1 \\ 1
    \end{pmatrix}.</md>
  </p>

  <p>For part (c), we are using our new form from today. We take the coefficient matrix multiply it by the vector of variables and get the last column of the augmented matrix:
    <md>\begin{pmatrix}
      1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1  \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
    \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x_{1} \\ x_{2} \\x_{3} \\ x_{4} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
      1 \\ 1\\ 1
    \end{pmatrix}.</md>
  </p>

  <p>For (d), my favorite is the matrix-vector form. This the most iconic one.</p>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the matrix
        <md>A = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
      <p>Consider the vectors
        <md>\vec{v}_{1} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ \end{pmatrix}, \vec{v}_{2} =\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\\end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Compute <m>\vec{v}_{1} \cdot \vec{v}_{2}</m></p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Compute <m>A \vec{v}_{1}</m>.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Compute <m>A \vec{v}_{2}</m></p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Compute <m>\vec{v}_{1} - A\vec{v}_{1}</m>.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Describe what multiplying by <m>A</m> does in words. For which vectors <m>\vec{v}</m> does <m>A \vec{v} = \vec{v}</m>?</p></statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <p>(a) You get
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\\end{pmatrix} = (-1)(-3) + (-1)(1) = 3-1 = 2.</md>
  </p>

  <p>(b) You get
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0(-1) +1(-1) \\ 1(-1) + 0(-1)\end{pmatrix}= \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -1
    \end{pmatrix}.</md>
  </p>

  <p>(c) You get
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0(-3) +1(1) \\ 1(-3) + 0(1)\end{pmatrix}= \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -3
    \end{pmatrix}.</md>
  </p>

  <p>(d) You get <m>(-1, -1) - (-1,-1) = (0,0)</m>.</p>

  <p>(e) Multiplying by <m>A</m> swaps the two coordinates. <m>A \vec{v} = \vec{v}</m> precisely when both coordinates have the same value.</p>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the following linear system defined by the matrix equation:
        <md>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp1 \amp 1 \\0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x_{1} \\x_{2} \\x_{3}\\x_{4} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\0\\ 0\\0 \end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement><p>What is the dimension of the solution set of the linear system?</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>What is the nullity of the matrix?</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>What is the rank of the matrix?</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>How does the span of the rows relate to the solution set?</p></statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <p>First the constraint matrix is in reduced row echelon form.</p>

  <p>(a) By the rank-nullity theorem, the dimension of the solution set of the system is the number of non-pivot columns, which is <m>2</m>.</p>

  <p>(b) The nullity is also the number of non-pivot columns, which is <m>2</m>.</p>

  <p>(c) The rank is the number of pivot columns, which is also <m>2</m>.</p>

  <p>(d) The span of the rows is the plane consisting of all vectors orthogonal to every vector in the solution set.</p>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider vectors <m>(1,1,0,0)</m> and <m>(0,0,1,1)</m>. Are they linearly independent? Describe their span as the solution set to a system of linear equations.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <p>To figure out if they are, put them as columns in a matrix
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}</md>
  </p>
  <p>You put this matrix in reduced row echelon form. To do this, I do replacement on the second and fourth rows by subtracting the first and third respectively from them to first get
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{pmatrix}</md>
  </p>
  <p>Then I do interchange to swap the second and third to get:
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{pmatrix}</md>
  </p>
  <p>Then every column is a pivot, so the columns of the original matrix are linearly independent. Hence, they are linearly independent.</p>

  <p>There is a generality method to figuring out their span using the orthogonality interpretation of subspaces.</p>

  <p>First, the plane orthogonal to these vectors is given by:
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp0 \amp 1 \amp1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} x_{1} \\ x_{2} \\ x_{3} \\x_{4} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0\\ \end{pmatrix}.</md>
  </p>
  <p>We want to solve this linear system. The augmented matrix of this system is
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp0 \amp 1 \amp1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix},</md>
    which is in reduced row echelon form. Then solving in terms of the free variables <m>x_{2}</m> and <m>x_{4}</m> yields the system is <m>x_{1} = -x_{2}</m> and <m>x_{3} = -x_{4}</m>, so it is
    <md>f(x_{2}, x_{4}) = (-x_{2}, x_{2},-x_{4},x_{4}) = x_{2}\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 1\\ 0\\0\end{pmatrix} + x_{4}\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\ -1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}.</md>
  </p>
  <p>Thus, we have now represented the orthogonal subspace as a span, and so the original subspace is the solution set to the linear system with rows given by the vectors that span <m>(-1,1,0,0)</m> and <m>(0,0,-1,1)</m>. That is, the linear system describing the span is
    <md>\begin{pmatrix} -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\0 \amp 0 \amp- 1  \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}x_{1} \\ x_{2} \\x_{3} \\x_{4} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.</md>
  </p>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>A</m> be a matrix, and suppose that the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent. Suppose that <m>A \vec{v} = A \vec{u}</m>. Then <m>A(\vec{u} -\vec{v}) = \vec{0}</m>. Explain why it must be that <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <p>Since the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, <m>A(\vec{u} -\vec{v}) = \vec{0}</m> implies that <m>\vec{u} - \vec{v} = \vec{0}</m> meaning that <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>.</p>
</section>
